<h1>Functions</h1>

<p>
  A function literal — a <em>lambda</em> — is written between braces:
  <code>{"{ |args| body }"}</code>. It's a value, just like a number or a
  string, so you can pass it to other functions.
</p>

<Code code={`[1, 2, 3].map { |n| n * 2 }`} replPaste mini />

<Code code={`[1, 2, 3].filter { |n| n > 1 }`} replPaste mini />

<p>
  <code>map</code> applies the lambda to every element; <code>filter</code>
  keeps the ones for which it returns true. Notice there are no loops here —
  you describe <em>what</em> to compute, and the list handles the iteration.
  Each call returns a fresh list; the original is never changed.
</p>

<p>
  When a lambda just calls one method on its argument, there's a shorthand:
  <code>&amp;.method</code> stands in for <code>{"{ |x| x.method }"}</code>.
  It's common enough that you'll see it in almost every pipeline.
</p>

<Code code={`[1, 2, 3].map(&.to(String))`} replPaste mini />

<p>
  You can also name functions, declare their types, and define them in
  multiple clauses that pattern-match on the arguments — which is exactly
  what the next lesson covers.
</p>
